# width: 120
# height: 40
# Ctrl+Shift+Tab is "previous editor" in VS Code and `buffer.prev`
# claims it in both profiles (`src/commands/specs.zig`). A terminal
# never sends `.tab` with shift, though: it sends its own back-tab code
# with the modifier dropped, and `tui/loop.zig`'s `translateKey` folds
# vaxis's form the same way. The spec parser used to build `.tab` +
# ctrl + shift, so the binding could not fire on any terminal — and the
# corpus could not see it, because a `.test` `key ctrl+shift+tab` went
# through `parseKeySpec` and synthesised the key a terminal never sends
# (hunt/findings-2026-09-21/kbd-ctrl-shift-tab-dead.md).
#
# `Key.canonical` is now the one place a shifted Tab is spelled, and
# `parseKeySpec` goes through it — so the two spellings below are the
# same chord, and both are the one the terminal delivers.
write a.txt "alpha\n"
write b.txt "bravo\n"
write c.txt "charlie\n"
open a.txt
open b.txt
open c.txt
expect pane "c.txt"

# Ctrl+Tab is fine — an unshifted Tab survives `translateKey` intact —
# so this is not "the harness cannot send a ctrl chord".
key ctrl+tab
expect pane "b.txt"
key ctrl+tab
expect pane "c.txt"

# The chord a VS Code user reaches for, as the terminal sends it.
key ctrl+backtab
expect pane "b.txt"
# …and as the spec spells it. Same chord now, so it walks on.
key ctrl+shift+tab
expect pane "a.txt"
# Wrapping back round proves it is `buffer.prev` and not a one-shot.
key ctrl+backtab
expect pane "c.txt"

# The vim profile's NvChad `<S-Tab>` bufferline-prev, revived by the
# same fold: bare backtab, no modifier.
command editor.use_vim
key backtab
expect pane "b.txt"
key shift+tab
expect pane "a.txt"
command editor.use_standard

# The Settings overlay is the same hole seen from the other side: it
# reads `.backtab` for "previous section", so before the fold a script's
# `shift+tab` stepped the section FORWARD. Both spellings step back now.
command view.settings
expect screen contains "UI · Editor · AI · Integrations · Reset"
expect screen contains "▸ Line numbers:"
key tab
expect screen contains "▸ Input style:"
key shift+tab
expect screen contains "▸ Line numbers:"
key tab
expect screen contains "▸ Input style:"
key backtab
expect screen contains "▸ Line numbers:"
key esc
